{
  "dom_id": "step-q346154",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189986/assets/185772b358771a524b784b06aee22a623d709b15b55d9e34000e2ef106b1e7d8.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ between the lower limit $x=1$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{x}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$",
